so the uk and some other european  countries are now dealing with their  worst ever monkey pox outbreak so what  do you need to know  this outbreak is really unusual most of  these cases have no connection to  west africa they haven't traveled  recently they've been within the uk but  they are picking up the infection here  and that makes it  slightly concerning for the uk health  security agency because they don't know  the source they don't know for sure how  it's spreading whether it is still in  the old way probably direct skin to skin  contact or whether there's a possibility  that there might be a mutation that  makes it easier to transmit through  coughs and sneezes and so on these are  unknowns  and that is the concern when there are  unknowns in the wake of a pandemic like  kovid it does cause people to start  wondering at the moment the risk to the  general public is low  but they are trying to make sure people  are aware of the symptoms so they come  forward if needed  the key symptoms of monkey pox are  flu-like symptoms to start with so  you've got the fever the muscle aches  and pains backache chills exhaustion but  also a very characteristic rash it looks  a little bit like chickenpox which  perhaps isn't surprising so it starts  off with as a rash which then blisters  and then scabs over over a period of two  to three weeks  well  it's likely to be most commonly spread  through direct skin to skin contact so  contact with the blisters the fluids  from those blisters and particularly  you've got a break in the skin but also  through bedding towels clothing there's  also a chance that it can be spread  through coughs and sneezes from somebody  who's got a very active  infection but that's less likely than  the direct skin to skin contact  one unusual aspect of this is that a  high proportion of the cases are gay or  bisexual men  not all  and it's important to say that this is  not a disease that's likely to be spread  by sex and it's likely to have nothing  to do with sexuality  but health authorities are trying to  raise awareness  in men that they need to be aware of the  symptoms because it may be  spreading within a sexual network men  who are having sex with other men who  may be at risk simply because  direct and intimate skin to skin contact  could be the root of transmission and  that's why they are trying to raise  awareness but that has to be balanced of  course against any potential for stigma  this could affect anybody but at the  moment they are very concerned about  spreading within the gay and bisexual  community in particular  one really interesting twist to this is  that doctors are dusting off vials of  smallpox vaccine now smallpox hasn't  been given as a jab routinely in the uk  since 1971 but there is a small  stockpile kept although it seems like a  completely different disease small parks  and monkey pox are in fact related and  the small fox vaccine is 85 effective  against monkey pox so the idea is that  not only can you give it to people who  haven't developed the symptoms yet but  you might also be able to give it to  people who have been in direct contact  and might be at risk  we are at the tail end of the covid  pandemic to see another animal virus  jumping into humans and then starting to  spread between people is obviously going  to set the alarm bells ringing for a lot  of folks now i think we need to be  careful at the moment we've got very few  cases spreading in an unusual way  so i think we need to be slightly  balanced on the one hand we do need to  find out what the source is understand  whether the virus has changed in any way  but we're not at the moment in another  covert situation this isn't a primarily  airborne disease and we do have an  effective vaccine there ready to go so  it's likely we will be able to shut this  down probably take a little bit longer  than it has in the past but i don't  think we're in the situation of another  pandemic
 

